{#
  summary.j2
  ==========
  One-paragraph summary of the scenario comparison.

  Context variables (from _build_summary_ctx):
    header               str   e.g. "SSP3, 2050"
    has_changes          bool  True when at least one driver produced text
    driver_list_str      str   e.g. "Population, Sanitation and Hydrology"
    outlook              str   'positive' | 'negative' | 'mixed'
    has_modelled_risk    bool  True when both scenarios have QMRA results
    risk_is_significant  bool
    risk_increased       bool
    risk_abs_delta       float relative % change in annual infection risk
    risk_confirms_drivers  bool|None  whether the QMRA agreed with the reading
                                      taken from the input drivers alone
#}
{{ header }}:
{% if not has_changes %}
No significant changes were detected in the modelled input drivers compared to the baseline scenario.
{% else %}
Key differences from baseline affect {{ driver_list_str }}.
{% endif %}
{% if has_modelled_risk and risk_is_significant %}
The quantitative microbial risk assessment confirms this as a net {{ risk_abs_delta | num }}% {{ "increase" if risk_increased else "decrease" }} in the annual probability of infection for an average resident.
{% if risk_confirms_drivers is sameas false %}
This runs counter to what the input drivers alone would suggest, indicating that the drivers do not simply add up: the effect of the dominant driver outweighs the rest.
{% endif %}
{% elif has_modelled_risk %}
The quantitative microbial risk assessment shows no material change in the annual probability of infection relative to the baseline.
{% elif outlook == 'positive' %}
On balance, the changes suggest a reduction in waterborne pathogen risk compared to the baseline scenario. No quantitative risk results are available to confirm this.
{% elif outlook == 'negative' %}
On balance, the changes suggest an increase in waterborne pathogen risk compared to the baseline scenario. No quantitative risk results are available to confirm this.
{% elif has_changes %}
The changes present mixed implications for waterborne pathogen risk, with risk-increasing and risk-reducing factors in roughly equal measure.
{% endif %}
